--TEST--
A call to an `abstract static` method is refused where the class it is called on is fixed while compiling — a written class name, and `self::` inside an anonymous function — because no override can be reached from there, `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
abstract class Page {
    public abstract static function title(): string;

    public static function heading(): tainted string {
        callable $f = fn (): string => self::title();
        return $f() as string;
    }
}

final class Blog extends Page {
    public static function title(): string {
        return "Blog";
    }
}

echo Page::title(), "\n";
// covers: lang:classes/inheritance
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0835]: `Page::title()` is `abstract`, so it has no body to call
  --> case.nvs:6:40
%A
error[E0835]: `Page::title()` is `abstract`, so it has no body to call
   --> case.nvs:17:6
%A
